# What `--emit-c` owes, and the two clang questions it must ask before it
# writes a byte (defect 048, panel 157, 2026-09-16).
#
# Split out of `cli/produce.hero` the day the work landed, when that file
# passed §11's 300 lines. **The seam is a concern and not a line count**:
# `produce` answers *how a program becomes a binary* and this answers *what the
# artifact of a stopped build owes*, which is a different question with a
# different verdict — `produce` may refuse a program, and this may only refuse
# the ARTIFACT.
#
# ## The defect this file is the repair for
#
# The write used to live in `cli/compile.hero`, which returned before
# `cli/produce.hero` ran at all. So the artifact was the FIRST emission: before
# the pointee check, which is a refusal, and before the tag round, which learns
# a spelling from clang's own words. `--emit-c` owes **the artifact of a build
# that PASSED**, and *C that clang accepts* is the weaker bar — five
# `tests/golden/fixedbugs/` cases satisfied it while writing over the stack.
#
# ## Two questions, and since 2026-09-17 one of them is not asked here
#
# **The pointee check REFUSES.** A binding declaring `@n: i32` against a header's
# `uint64_t *` emitted at exit 0, compiled clean under the real flag list, and
# wrote `0xAAAAAAAA` over the next stack slot — design.md §1.12, measured. Its
# *no* is the whole point, so it must be able to say it.
#
# **The tag's spelling is the ROUNDS' answer, handed in.** `cli/produce.hero`
# runs the build's rounds first; when clang says *must use 'struct' tag* the
# word is learned and the round runs again, and only a build that PASSED reaches
# `write_c`, which re-renders with what was learned. So `--emit-c` refuses
# exactly what `build` refuses — a missing header, a binding the header refutes
# — and there is no longer any C for a program that has no build. That retires
# the sentence this file carried for a day, *fourteen `fixedbugs/` bindings exist
# precisely to have C where a build has none*: they have their refusal instead,
# which is what panel 157 R1 said an artifact owes (defect 049).
#
# ## Why clang and not a rule
#
# Four header shapes, compiled at the sitting: `struct X *` fails on a typedef
# of an ANONYMOUS struct, the bare word fails on a tag-only header, and a
# typedef shim at the top of the artifact fails on the anonymous shape too. No
# fixed spelling is correct. The only clang-free route left is a C declaration
# parser inside Heroes, which the compiler seat's veto is aimed at.
#
# ## The one-way edge
#
# This file does the work and returns a verdict; `cli/produce.hero` decides
# whose fault a failure is, because `blamed` lives there and is the round's too.
# Heroes refuses module cycles, so the arrow pointing one way is the condition
# under which this seam exists at all.

use source
use emit
use cli/io
use cli/compile
use cli/toolchain
use cli/pointee
use cli/pointee_ask

# The artifact, written or printed, AFTER the build's own rounds have passed. A
# failure here is the pointee check's and carries its message up so the caller
# can decide whose it is — this file never blames anybody.
#
# `struct_tags` is what those rounds learned from clang — the handles whose
# tag the header spells with `struct` in front — and `version` is the build's,
# because `emit_maybe` stamps it. Both come from `cli/produce.hero`, which owns
# the rounds; this file only applies their answer.
function write_c(t: toolchain.Toolchain, options: compile.Options, art: compile.Artifact, s: source.Source, @answered: [pointee_ask.Answered], source_dir: str, package_flags: [str], struct_tags: {str: bool}, version: str) -> ()?
    checked = pointee.check(
        t,
        art.program,
        art.checked,
        s,
        art.decls,
        answered: @answered,
        search_include: options.headers_from,
        package_flags: package_flags,
        source_dir: source_dir
    )
    checked?

    # **THE ARTIFACT IS THE C THAT COMPILED, NOT THE FIRST DRAFT** (defect 049,
    # closing panel 157 R1 the way it was written). Until 2026-09-17 this ran
    # BEFORE the rounds, so a handle whose header spells `struct X` was written
    # as the bare word clang had just refused — and `run-fixedbugs-getaddrinfo-
    # is-bindable`'s blessed artifact carried `addrinfo *` seven times where
    # `heroes build` compiled `struct addrinfo *`.
    #
    # A probe lived here for one commit and was withdrawn: it asked clang the
    # same question SEPARATELY, and on a machine without the header it learned
    # nothing and emitted the bare word at exit 0, where `build` on that machine
    # says `error[ffi_missing_header]` at exit 1. So the artifact differed
    # between two machines and `emission` compared one against the other. The
    # probe's error was to decide alone what the rounds and `blamed` already
    # decide together: `--emit-c` owes the artifact of a build that PASSED, so
    # on the machine without the header there is no artifact and the build's own
    # refusal is the answer — which `emission` already knows to skip. The
    # artifact is CONDITIONAL on the machine, never DEPENDENT on it.
    #
    # `emit_maybe` with the learned tags is exactly what `assemble.fused`
    # compiles, so the bytes written are the bytes clang accepted. With no
    # tagged handle the map is empty and the text is untouched.
    text: str @ art.c

    if struct_tags.len() > 0
        text @ emit.emit_maybe(target: options.target, p: art.program, c: art.checked, s: s, decls: art.decls, version: version, text: true, struct_tags: struct_tags).c

    if options.output.is_err()
        io.print_artifact(text: text)
        return ok()
    written = write_file(path: options.output.must(), text: text)

    if written.is_err()
        io.eprint(text: "error: cannot write " + options.output.must())
        return fail(code: "failed", msg: "cannot write the artifact")

    return ok()
